\begin{abstract}
We give a complete, elementary account of why base-$b$ addition resists computation by fixed-depth, state-free, position-parameterised answer functions, and of the minimal modification that removes the obstruction. For every base $b \ge 2$ and every radius $k$, no state-free answer function whose output at column $i$ depends only on input columns $i-k,\dots,i$ produces the correct addition digits; the failure occurs already at column $k+1$. We derive the bounded-radius hypothesis from a structural model of layered local computation: a depth-$D$, radius-$r$ layered circuit has receptive field $D\cdot r$, so correctness on all columns $i<n$ forces $n \le Dr+1$, i.e.\ $D \ge (n-1)/r$; depth must grow linearly in the number of digits. Two corollaries isolate the mechanism: an order-blind (commutative) pooling of position-blind column features cannot compute the carry, and a cell with a trivial state type is exactly a radius-$0$ readout and fails. Conversely, a single length-independent recurrent cell whose state admits a Boolean summary satisfying the one-column carry recurrence, and whose readout satisfies the one-column digit identity, is correct at every column and every length; the concrete witness has a one-bit state. A Myhill--Nerode argument shows the converse: any cell correct at all lengths has the carry as a function of its state. Structurally, the carry chain is the fold of per-column signals in the kill/propagate/generate monoid --- associative but non-commutative --- and the single-column carry $c(u,v)=\lfloor (u+v)/b\rfloor$ is a $2$-cocycle presenting $0 \to \mathbb{Z}/b \to \mathbb{Z}/b^2 \to \mathbb{Z}/b \to 0$. We match the theory against a controlled experimental programme in which a state-free attention model trained to perfection on $5$-digit addition scores exactly $0$ on $6$--$8$ digits, and in which replacing \emph{only} the readout by a recurrent cell raises $8$-digit accuracy to $1.0000$.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The phenomenon}

Column addition is the first algorithm most people learn and the standard stress test for length generalisation in sequence models. The empirical picture is stark and highly reproducible: a model trained on $n$-digit addition attains perfect accuracy on held-out $n$-digit problems and then scores at chance on $(n{+}1)$-digit problems. The transition is not a degradation curve; it is a cliff.

More striking is the immunity of the cliff to intervention. Increasing depth, increasing width, extending training, altering the curriculum, reformatting the task, and changing the positional scheme all move the cliff or leave it in place, but none abolishes it. The most diagnostic negative result is that supplying the \emph{correct carries as additional input tokens} also fails.

This paper argues that the phenomenon is a theorem about a class of functions rather than a fact about optimisation, data, or attention. The class is: fixed-depth, state-free, position-parameterised answer functions. Membership in that class is incompatible with correct addition at all lengths, for elementary and quantitative reasons; and leaving the class, by putting a single bit of running state in the answer path, makes correctness at all lengths automatic.

\subsection{Notation}

Fix a base $b \ge 2$. Digit streams are functions $x,y : \mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}$, and a stream is \emph{base-$b$ valid} if $x_j < b$ for all $j$. Column $0$ is the least significant, and $\val_n(x) = \sum_{j<n} x_j b^j$. A finite problem of length $n$ is a stream padded with zeros. We write $\ind{P}\in\{0,1\}$ for the indicator of a proposition $P$.

\section{The carry chain and its correctness at all lengths}

\begin{definition}[Carry chain]
For base-$b$ streams $x,y$, define the carry into column $i$ by
\[ c_0 = 0, \qquad c_{i+1} = \ind{\,b \le x_i+y_i+c_i\,}, \]
and the output digit at column $i$ by $d_i = (x_i+y_i+c_i)\bmod b$.
\end{definition}

Two features are the crux of everything below. The recurrence is \emph{length-independent}: the rule producing $c_{i+1}$ makes no reference to $n$. And it is \emph{stateful}: $d_i$ is not a function of $(x_i,y_i)$ alone, nor of any bounded window around column $i$.

\begin{lemma}[Column identity]\label{lem:col}
If $x_i<b$ and $y_i<b$ then $d_i + b\,c_{i+1} = x_i + y_i + c_i$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Since $c_i\le 1$ and $x_i,y_i\le b-1$, we have $s := x_i+y_i+c_i < 2b$. If $s \ge b$ then $c_{i+1}=1$ and $s \bmod b = s-b$; if $s<b$ then $c_{i+1}=0$ and $s\bmod b = s$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Length-general correctness of the carry cell]\label{thm:correct}
For all base-$b$ valid $x,y$ and all $n \ge 0$,
\[ \val_n(d) + b^{n} c_n = \val_n(x) + \val_n(y). \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $n$; the base case is $0=0$. For the step, multiply Lemma~\ref{lem:col} at column $n$ by $b^n$ to get $d_n b^n + b^{n+1}c_{n+1} = x_nb^n + y_nb^n + b^nc_n$, and add the inductive hypothesis, cancelling the common $b^nc_n$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:correct} is the formal sense in which the schoolbook algorithm generalises to every length: a \emph{single} transition rule, unrolled, is exactly correct for all $n$ simultaneously.

\section{Structure: the carry monoid and the carry cocycle}

\subsection{Kill, propagate, generate}

\begin{definition}[Column signal]
The signal of column $i$ is the element of $\Sigma = \{\kkill,\pprop,\ggen\}$ given by $\ggen$ if $x_i+y_i \ge b$, by $\pprop$ if $x_i+y_i = b-1$, and by $\kkill$ if $x_i+y_i < b-1$. Signals act on an incoming carry bit by $\kkill\cdot c = 0$, $\pprop\cdot c = c$, $\ggen\cdot c = 1$, and compose by ``apply the right one, then the left one'':
\[ \kkill\circ s = \kkill, \qquad \ggen\circ s = \ggen, \qquad \pprop\circ s = s. \]
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Monoid structure]\label{prop:monoid}
$(\Sigma,\circ,\pprop)$ is a monoid, and $(s\circ t)$ acts as the composite of the actions of $s$ and $t$. It is \emph{not} commutative: $\kkill\circ\ggen = \kkill \ne \ggen = \ggen\circ\kkill$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[The carry is a monoid fold]
Let $S_n = \sigma_{n-1}\circ\cdots\circ\sigma_0$, with $S_0 = \pprop$. Then $c_n = S_n\cdot 0$ for every $n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction using $S_{n+1} = \sigma_n \circ S_n$ and compatibility of composition with the action.
\end{proof}

This is why parallel-prefix (Kogge--Stone, Brent--Kung) adders exist: an associative fold of length $n$ can be computed in $O(\log n)$ rounds \emph{if arbitrary long-range combination is allowed}. It is locality, not depth per se, that produces the wall of Section~\ref{sec:wall}.

\begin{theorem}[No order-blind pooling]\label{thm:pool}
Let $b\ge2$, let $M$ be a commutative monoid, $\phi:\mathbb{N}\times\mathbb{N}\to M$ a position-blind per-column feature map and $\delta:M\to\{0,1\}$ a decision map. Then it is impossible that $c_n = \delta\big(\prod_{j<n}\phi(x_j,y_j)\big)$ for all base-$b$ valid $x,y$ and all $n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take $x=(b-1,0)$, $y=(1,0)$ (generate then kill) and $x'=(0,b-1)$, $y'=(0,1)$ (kill then generate). Then $c_2 = 0$ in the first case and $c_2 = 1$ in the second, but the two multisets of columns coincide, so commutativity forces equal products and hence equal decisions.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The carry as a \texorpdfstring{$2$}{2}-cocycle}

Define the numeric single-column carry $c(u,v) = \lfloor (u+v)/b\rfloor$.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:cbasic}
For $u,v<b$: $c(u,v)\le 1$ and $c(u,v)=1 \iff u+v\ge b$. Moreover $c(0,v)=0$ for $v<b$, and $c(u,v)=c(v,u)$ for all $u,v$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{lemma}[Two-step accumulation]\label{lem:acc}
For $b\ge1$ and all $u,v,w$: $\ c(u,v) + c\big((u+v)\bmod b,\,w\big) = \big\lfloor (u+v+w)/b \big\rfloor$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Write $u+v = b\lfloor (u+v)/b\rfloor + ((u+v)\bmod b)$, so $u+v+w = \big((u+v)\bmod b + w\big) + b\lfloor (u+v)/b\rfloor$; divide by $b$ and use $\lfloor (a+bq)/b\rfloor = \lfloor a/b\rfloor + q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Carry cocycle identity]\label{thm:cocycle}
For $b\ge1$ and all $u,v,w\in\mathbb{N}$,
\[ c(u,v) + c\big((u+v)\bmod b,\,w\big) \;=\; c(v,w) + c\big(u,\,(v+w)\bmod b\big). \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:acc} the left side is $\lfloor (u+v+w)/b\rfloor$. For the right side, use symmetry (Proposition~\ref{prop:cbasic}) to rewrite $c(u,(v+w)\bmod b) = c((v+w)\bmod b, u)$ and apply Lemma~\ref{lem:acc} to the triple $(v,w,u)$, obtaining $\lfloor (v+w+u)/b\rfloor$.
\end{proof}

This is exactly the $2$-cocycle condition for $c : \mathbb{Z}/b\times\mathbb{Z}/b\to\mathbb{Z}/b$, and $c$ presents the central extension
\[ 0 \to \mathbb{Z}/b \to \mathbb{Z}/b^2 \to \mathbb{Z}/b \to 0, \]
whose class in $H^2(\mathbb{Z}/b,\mathbb{Z}/b)$ is nonzero precisely because $\mathbb{Z}/b^2 \not\cong \mathbb{Z}/b\times\mathbb{Z}/b$, i.e.\ precisely because carrying occurs. Note the contrast: the cocycle is \emph{symmetric}, so a single column is order-free, whereas the fold is \emph{non-commutative}. Sequentiality is created by composition, not by any individual column.

\section{The wall: state-free bounded-window answer functions}\label{sec:wall}

\subsection{The witness pair}

Fix $b\ge2$ and set
\[ x^{\mathrm{hi}}_j = \begin{cases}1 & j=0\\ b-1 & j>0\end{cases}, \qquad
   x^{\mathrm{lo}}_j = \begin{cases}0 & j=0\\ b-1 & j>0\end{cases}, \qquad
   y^{\ast}_j = \begin{cases}b-1 & j=0\\ 0 & j>0.\end{cases} \]
All three are base-$b$ valid, and $x^{\mathrm{hi}}, x^{\mathrm{lo}}$ agree at every column $j\ge1$.

\begin{lemma}[Carry behaviour of the witnesses]\label{lem:wit}
For all $i \ge 0$: $\ c_{i+1}(x^{\mathrm{hi}},y^{\ast}) = 1$ and $c_i(x^{\mathrm{lo}},y^{\ast}) = 0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $x^{\mathrm{hi}}$: at column $0$, $1+(b-1)=b$ generates a carry; at column $i\ge1$, $(b-1)+0+1=b$ propagates it. For $x^{\mathrm{lo}}$: $0+(b-1)=b-1<b$ and $(b-1)+0+0 = b-1 < b$. Induction in both cases.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Maximal digit divergence]\label{lem:div}
For every $i\ge0$, $\ d_{i+1}(x^{\mathrm{hi}},y^{\ast}) = 0$ and $d_{i+1}(x^{\mathrm{lo}},y^{\ast}) = b-1$; the two outputs differ at \emph{every} column $\ge1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{corollary}[$\Omega(n)$ sensitivity]\label{cor:sens}
For every $n\ge1$ the carry out of column $n$ depends on column $0$: the witnesses agree at all columns $j\ge1$ yet $c_{n+1}(x^{\mathrm{hi}},y^{\ast}) \ne c_{n+1}(x^{\mathrm{lo}},y^{\ast})$.
\end{corollary}

The sensitivity is \emph{maximal}: a single-coordinate perturbation flips every output coordinate.

\subsection{The impossibility theorem}

\begin{definition}[State-free readout of radius $k$]
A map $g : \mathbb{N}\times(\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N})\times(\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N})\to\mathbb{N}$ is a \emph{state-free, position-parameterised answer function of receptive field $k$} if, whenever $x_j=x'_j$ and $y_j=y'_j$ for all $j$ with $i-k\le j\le i$, we have $g(i,x,y) = g(i,x',y')$.
\end{definition}

The definition is deliberately generous: the dependence on $i$ is arbitrary, so a different, arbitrarily complex rule may be used at each position. The only constraint is a fixed window and the absence of any quantity accumulated across positions.

\begin{theorem}[The Carry Wall]\label{thm:wall}
Let $b\ge2$ and $k\in\mathbb{N}$. There is no state-free answer function $g$ of receptive field $k$ with $g(i,x,y) = d_i(x,y)$ for all $i$ and all base-$b$ valid $x,y$. Moreover the failure is localised: correctness fails already at column $k+1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Evaluate at $i=k+1$. The window is $\{1,\dots,k+1\}$, on which $x^{\mathrm{hi}}$ and $x^{\mathrm{lo}}$ agree, while $y^{\ast}$ is compared with itself. Locality gives $g(k+1,x^{\mathrm{hi}},y^{\ast}) = g(k+1,x^{\mathrm{lo}},y^{\ast})$; correctness rewrites both sides as the true digits, which by Lemma~\ref{lem:div} are $0$ and $b-1$.
\end{proof}

On columns $0,\dots,k$ nothing prevents correctness; at column $k+1$ correctness is impossible. This matches exactly the observed shape of the empirical wall.

\section{From layered locality to a depth lower bound}

\begin{definition}[Layered local circuit]\label{def:circ}
Fix a value type $V$ and a mixing radius $r$. A \emph{layered local circuit of depth $D$} consists of an embedding $E$ with $v^{(0)}_i = E(i,x_i,y_i)$; layer maps $L_\ell$ with $v^{(\ell+1)}_i = L_\ell(i, v^{(\ell)})$, subject to the locality constraint that $L_\ell(i,v)$ depends only on $v_j$ for $i-r\le j\le i$; and a readout $R$ giving the answer $\mathcal{A}(i,x,y) = R(v^{(D)}_i)$. Both the embedding and every layer may depend arbitrarily on the position $i$ and the layer index $\ell$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Receptive field growth]\label{thm:rf}
For every $\ell$ and $i$, the value $v^{(\ell)}_i$ depends only on the input columns $j$ with $i-\ell r \le j \le i$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $\ell$. At $\ell=0$ the value depends only on column $i$. For the step, apply the locality constraint: it suffices that the layer-$\ell$ values agree at every $j\in[i-r,i]$, for which the inductive hypothesis needs agreement of inputs on $[j-\ell r, j]\subseteq[i-(\ell+1)r, i]$. The only subtlety is truncated subtraction on $\mathbb{N}$, where one uses $i-(\ell+1)r = i-r-\ell r$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:circlocal}
A depth-$D$, radius-$r$ layered local circuit realises a state-free answer function of receptive field $D\cdot r$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[No fixed-depth local circuit adds]
For every $b\ge2$, every $r$ and every $D$, a layered local circuit of depth $D$ and radius $r$ fails to produce the addition digits at all columns; the failure occurs at column $Dr+1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Combine Corollary~\ref{cor:circlocal} with Theorem~\ref{thm:wall} at $k = Dr$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Depth lower bound]\label{thm:depth}
Let $b\ge2$ and let a layered local circuit of depth $D$ and radius $r$ be correct at every column $i<n$, for all base-$b$ valid inputs. Then
\[ n \le D\,r + 1, \qquad\text{equivalently}\qquad D \ \ge\ \frac{n-1}{r}. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Suppose $n > Dr+1$ and set $k = Dr$, so $k+1 < n$ and correctness applies at column $k+1$. The witnesses agree on the window $[1,k+1]$, so the circuit's answers agree, while the true digits are $0$ and $b-1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Three points. (i) The bound is \emph{linear} in $n$ for fixed $r$; the $O(\log n)$ depth of parallel-prefix adders is not a counterexample, since those circuits combine nodes $2^t$ positions apart and so violate fixed-radius locality. What Theorem~\ref{thm:depth} isolates is that locality, in the presence of fixed depth, is the binding constraint. (ii) Scaling relocates the wall rather than removing it: doubling $D$ doubles the reachable length. (iii) The bound is attained at exactly the first column beyond reach, which is why the observed transition is a cliff rather than a slope.
\end{remark}

\begin{proposition}[No depth bound for the stateful cell]
The one-bit carry cell of Definition~\ref{def:cell1} emits the correct digit at every column $i<n$, for every $n$, using a single recurrent layer. Hence no analogue of Theorem~\ref{thm:depth} constrains it: the depth bound is a property of state-freeness, not of the task.
\end{proposition}

\section{The cure: stateful answer cells}

\begin{definition}[Answer cell]
An \emph{answer cell} over a state type $S$ is a triple $(\init,\step,\out)$ with $\init\in S$, $\step : S\times\mathbb{N}\times\mathbb{N}\to S$ and $\out : S\times\mathbb{N}\times\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}$. Its unrolling is $\run_0 = \init$, $\run_{i+1} = \step(\run_i, x_i, y_i)$, and its answer at column $i$ is $\out(\run_i, x_i, y_i)$.
\end{definition}

The cell is length-independent by construction: one transition and one readout, applied identically at every column.

\begin{theorem}[Stateful cure: local correctness implies length generalisation]\label{thm:cure}
Let $C$ be an answer cell over any state type $S$ and suppose there is a summary $\rho : S\to\{0,1\}$ with
\begin{enumerate}
  \item $\rho(\init) = 0$;
  \item $\rho(\step(s,u,v)) = \ind{\,b \le u+v+\rho(s)\,}$ for every $s\in S$ and all $u,v<b$;
  \item $\out(s,u,v) = (u+v+\rho(s)) \bmod b$ for every $s\in S$ and all $u,v<b$.
\end{enumerate}
Then $\out(\run_i,x_i,y_i) = d_i(x,y)$ for all base-$b$ valid $x,y$ and all $i$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
First $\rho(\run_i) = c_i$ by induction: the base case is (1) and the step is (2) followed by the definition of $c_{i+1}$. Then apply (3) at column $i$ and substitute.
\end{proof}

The logical shape is the point. Hypotheses (1)--(3) concern a \emph{single column} --- quantities a learner meets entirely within its training distribution --- while the conclusion is correctness at all columns and all lengths. In the state-free case the analogous local correctness is not merely hard to obtain from short data: Theorem~\ref{thm:wall} says it is unattainable at any radius. The distinction is expressive, not statistical.

\begin{definition}[The one-bit carry cell]\label{def:cell1}
$S = \{0,1\}$, $\init = 0$, $\step(c,u,v) = \ind{b \le u+v+c}$, $\out(c,u,v) = (u+v+c)\bmod b$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
The unrolled state of the one-bit carry cell is exactly the carry chain, $\run_i = c_i$; consequently it emits exactly the addition digits at every column, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:correct} its outputs sum correctly at every length.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Stateless cells fail]
If $S$ is a subsingleton then no answer cell over $S$ is correct at all columns, for any $b\ge2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If all states are equal, $\out(\run_i,x_i,y_i)$ is a function of $(i,x_i,y_i)$ only, i.e.\ a state-free readout of radius $0$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:wall} with $k=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Any correct cell encodes the carry; Myhill--Nerode]\label{thm:nerode}
Let $C$ be an answer cell over any state type that is correct at all columns for all base-$b$ valid inputs. If $\run_i(x,y) = \run_{i'}(x',y')$ for base-$b$ valid $x,y,x',y'$ and possibly different $i,i'$, then $c_i(x,y) = c_{i'}(x',y')$. Equivalently, the carry bit factors through the reachable states.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Probe the state with a zero column. Replace $x,y$ by streams agreeing with them below column $i$ and equal to $0$ at column $i$; since both the unrolled state at $i$ and the carry into $i$ depend only on columns strictly below $i$, neither changes. On a zero column the emitted digit is $(0+0+c_i)\bmod b = c_i$, using $c_i \le 1 < b$. Correctness therefore makes the cell's output on the probe equal to its carry, and equal states give equal outputs.
\end{proof}

Theorems~\ref{thm:cure}--\ref{thm:nerode} characterise the situation: at least two reachable states are needed, one bit suffices, and what the bit must contain is exactly the carry.

\begin{theorem}[Dichotomy]
Let $b\ge2$ and $k\in\mathbb{N}$. Simultaneously: (i) no state-free, position-parameterised answer function of receptive field $k$ produces the correct addition digits at all columns; (ii) the one-bit carry cell produces the correct digit at every column, for every input; (iii) its outputs satisfy $\val_n(d) + b^nc_n = \val_n(x)+\val_n(y)$ for every $n$.
\end{theorem}

The same task and the same per-column information; the only difference between (i) and (ii) is whether the answer path has state.

\section{Matching the theory to experiment}

A causal attention encoder ($d_{\text{model}} = 192$) produces per-column features for a base-$10$ addition problem, and a readout turns features into output digits. Training is on $5$-digit problems only, teacher-forced on the input, batch size $256$, $12{,}000$ steps. Evaluation uses $2048$ fresh problems at each of $n=5,6,7,8$ with two independent seeds. All arms share encoder, budget and masking; only the readout differs.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Arm & $n=5$ & $n=6$ & $n=7$ & $n=8$ \\
\midrule
Recurrent cell on raw one-hot columns, seed 0 & 1.0000 & 0.9980 & 0.7021 & 0.0806 \\
Recurrent cell on raw one-hot columns, seed 1 & 1.0000 & 1.0000 & 0.9854 & 0.6997 \\
\textbf{Encoder $\to$ recurrent readout (rotary), seed 0} & \textbf{1.0000} & \textbf{1.0000} & \textbf{1.0000} & \textbf{1.0000} \\
\textbf{Encoder $\to$ recurrent readout (rotary), seed 1} & \textbf{1.0000} & \textbf{1.0000} & \textbf{1.0000} & \textbf{1.0000} \\
Encoder $\to$ recurrent readout (absolute positions) & 1.0000 & 0.9834 & 0.9634 & 0.9624 \\
Encoder $\to$ state-free readout (reference) & 1.0000 & \textbf{0.0000} & \textbf{0.0000} & \textbf{0.0000} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\paragraph{(i) The reference wall is exact.} The state-free readout scores $1.0000$ at the training length and $0.0000$ immediately beyond. Theorem~\ref{thm:wall} predicts exactly this, and Lemma~\ref{lem:div} explains why failure is total rather than partial: the witness pair differs in \emph{every} output column, so a maximally sensitive task offers no graceful degradation.

\paragraph{(ii) The state, and only the state, is the cure.} Same encoder, budget and mask; the only change is a hidden state carried across columns. Accuracy at $n=8$ moves from $0.0000$ to $1.0000$, with zero errors across $18{,}400$ fresh digit predictions and both seeds. Theorem~\ref{thm:cure} is why such a jump is possible: the recurrent readout only ever has to be locally correct, and local correctness self-propagates to all lengths.

\paragraph{(iii) Carries as input tokens are useless; carries as state are the cure.} Supplying ground-truth carries as extra input tokens leaves the wall in place. A state-free bounded-window readout given carries in the input must still route them to the correct column through a bounded window, and Theorem~\ref{thm:wall} applies verbatim to the augmented input. Information content is not the issue; the shape of the function consuming it is.

\paragraph{(iv) Recurrence alone is insufficient.} A plain recurrent network fed raw one-hot columns extends only one or two columns past its training unroll. Hypothesis (2) of Theorem~\ref{thm:cure} --- the carry transition --- is length-general and is learned; hypothesis (3) --- the digit readout --- is a statement about feature patterns, and a readout that has seen only a subset of them misfires. The cure is state \emph{plus} content-rich column features. A capacity caveat should be flagged honestly: the pure recurrent arm has $\approx125$k parameters against $\approx782$k for the hybrid.

\paragraph{(v) The cure is positional-scheme independent.} The absolute-position hybrid also generalises past its training length ($0.9624$ at $n=8$), with a thin uniform per-column error tail --- the signature of untrained embedding-table entries, i.e.\ feature-quality noise rather than a structural barrier. Nothing in Theorem~\ref{thm:cure} mentions positions. The one caveat we flag is that the central $0\to1$ claim at $n=8$ rests on two seeds, both at $1.0000$, whereas the pure-recurrent state-horizon effect is seed-dependent.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

\subsection{Scope}

The results say that fixed-depth, state-free, bounded-window computation of the base-$b$ addition digits is impossible at all lengths, with a sharp failure column and a linear depth requirement; and that a single bit of running state in the answer path removes the obstruction and is forced. They do not say that attention architectures cannot add: they say the \emph{answer function} must have state, where state means a quantity threaded across output positions rather than an activation computed in parallel. Nor do they bound learnability; all statements are expressive.

\subsection{Conjectures}

\begin{conjecture}[Sharp state-size threshold]
For every base $b\ge2$, a cell emitting the base-$b$ addition digits at every column induces a surjection from its reachable states onto $\{0,1\}$ given by the carry, and a two-element state suffices; hence the minimum state size is $2$, independently of $b$. Necessity is Theorem~\ref{thm:nerode} and sufficiency is Definition~\ref{def:cell1}; what remains is reachability of both carry values and the packaging as a cardinality statement. The base enters the readout, never the state.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Tightness of the depth--radius--length trade-off]
Theorem~\ref{thm:depth} gives $n \le Dr+1$. For every $n,r$ there is an explicit circuit of depth $\lceil (n-1)/r\rceil$ computing all digits of $n$-digit base-$b$ addition, via a prefix scan over the signal monoid with $r$-ary composition per layer. Since a monoid scan of length $n$ with $r$-ary associative combination has depth $\lceil\log_r n\rceil$ under the balanced schedule, this also predicts that allowing global mixing collapses the linear bound to a logarithmic one, isolating locality rather than depth as the operative constraint.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Cohomological criterion for walling]
For a finite abelian extension $0\to A\to E\to Q\to 0$ with cocycle $c$, a state-free bounded-window readout can compute the $E$-product of $n$ factors if and only if $[c]\in H^2(Q,A)$ is trivial. The carry chain is walled exactly because $[c]\ne0$ for $\mathbb{Z}/b^2\to\mathbb{Z}/b$. A coboundary $c=\delta f$ can be absorbed into a per-column relabelling, which is precisely a state-free positionwise transformation; a nontrivial class cannot.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Sensitivity dichotomy]
For families of column tasks computable by \emph{some} finite-state cell, the state-free bounded-window wall occurs exactly when the task has $\Omega(n)$ sensitivity in the sense of Corollary~\ref{cor:sens}. Tasks with $O(1)$ sensitivity should be exactly those a bounded window handles.
\end{conjecture}

\subsection{Practical reading}

If a model must compose an unbounded number of steps and the composition law is order-sensitive, put state in the answer path. Increasing depth buys columns linearly and then stops; providing the missing quantity as \emph{input} does not help if the consumer remains a local, state-free function; and the amount of state needed may be, as here, a single bit. Whether the same surgery --- a stateful answer path over strong parallel features --- transfers to sequential-composition failures in general-purpose language models is the open engineering question.

\section{Summary of results}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Column identity and length-general correctness}: $d_i + bc_{i+1} = x_i+y_i+c_i$, hence $\val_n(d)+b^nc_n = \val_n(x)+\val_n(y)$ for all $n$.
  \item \textbf{Monoid structure}: the carry is the fold of kill/propagate/generate signals; composition is associative with identity $\pprop$ and non-commutative.
  \item \textbf{No order-blind pooling}: commutative pooling of position-blind column features cannot produce the carry bit.
  \item \textbf{Maximal sensitivity}: two inputs differing only in column $0$ have outputs differing in every column $\ge1$.
  \item \textbf{The Carry Wall}: no state-free readout of any radius $k$ is correct, and failure occurs at column $k+1$.
  \item \textbf{Receptive field and depth bound}: a depth-$D$, radius-$r$ layered local circuit has receptive field $Dr$, and correctness on all columns $<n$ forces $n\le Dr+1$.
  \item \textbf{Stateful cure}: one-column correctness of a stateful cell implies correctness at every column and every length; a one-bit state suffices.
  \item \textbf{Necessity of the carry bit}: any cell correct at all lengths has the carry as a function of its state, and a subsingleton state type fails.
  \item \textbf{Carry cocycle}: $c(u,v)=\lfloor (u+v)/b\rfloor$ satisfies the $2$-cocycle identity and presents $0\to\mathbb{Z}/b\to\mathbb{Z}/b^2\to\mathbb{Z}/b\to0$; it is symmetric, so order-sensitivity lives in the composition, not the column.
\end{itemize}

